@meta
id: 02-03
title: Abandoning changes on purpose
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: quit, write
par: 79
minutes: 5
vscode: closing the tab and clicking "Don't Save"
open: deploy-checklist.md

@brief
`:q` protects you from losing work by accident. Sometimes losing the work
is the point: you went down a path, it was wrong, and the version already
on disk is better than anything in front of you. `:q!` is how you say so.
The exclamation mark means "yes, I know, do it anyway".

This is the move that makes vim safe to experiment in. Save a good state,
try something reckless, and if it goes badly the good state is one command
away.

Work through this in order, exactly as written.

  1. Step 2 of the checklist is incomplete. It should read:

         2. Run the migration on the read replica first

     Fix it, and write the file.

  2. Now start a detour. You decide step 4 is really three steps, and you
     begin by noting it on the line:

         4. Watch the error rate for ten minutes (split this into three)

  3. Then remember the ticket said nothing about splitting step 4, and the
     checklist is being read off a screen during a deploy in an hour. The
     detour goes. Not the migration fix — that was right, and you wrote it.

Get out of vim leaving the file exactly as it was at your write.

@keys
| `:q!` | quit, throwing away everything since the last write |
| `ZQ` | the same thing in two keys |
| `:e!` | reload the file from disk, discarding changes, and stay in it |

@hints
Step 1 ends with `:w`. That write is what makes the rest of this safe.
--
After the detour, nothing you want is in the buffer that is not already
on disk. So do not save again.
--
`:q` will refuse, because the buffer has changed since the write. `:q!`
will not.

@solution
    jjj $ a  on the read replica first <Esc> :w<Enter>
    jj $ a  (split this into three) <Esc>
    :q!<Enter>

The file on disk has the migration fix and no trace of the detour,
because `:q!` discards the buffer, and the buffer is the only place the
detour ever existed.

The habit this unlocks is worth more than the command. Write when you are
happy, experiment freely after that, and `:q!` when the experiment fails.
You are using the file on disk as a one-step save point.

`:e!` is the same idea without leaving: it rereads the file from disk and
throws away everything since the last write, and you carry on editing
from there. Use it when the detour was wrong but you still have work to do
in the file.

One warning. `:q!` is indiscriminate. It does not care that the first
thing you did was good and the last thing was bad — if both are unwritten,
both go. The rest of this chapter is about the tool that can tell them
apart.

@start deploy-checklist.md
# Deploy checklist: payments 2.14

1. Drain the refund queue
2. Run the migration
3. Flip the feature flag
4. Watch the error rate for ten minutes

@expected deploy-checklist.md
# Deploy checklist: payments 2.14

1. Drain the refund queue
2. Run the migration on the read replica first
3. Flip the feature flag
4. Watch the error rate for ten minutes
